2. Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)
What it is: Automatically scales pods up or down based on CPU/memory usage. 4
Why used: When traffic spikes (e.g., e-commerce app from 10 to 10,000 users), a fixed pod count cannot survive — HPA dynamically adds or removes pods. 5
Two types of scaling:
Horizontal — increases the number of pods. 6
Vertical — increases resources of existing pods. 6
How it works:
Metric Server monitors CPU percentages continuously. 7
When CPU crosses the threshold, HPA creates new pods. 89
When load drops, pods are released automatically. 10
Real-world tip: Production threshold is typically 70% CPU; 2% is demo-only. 111213
Commands:
kubectl autoscale deployment nginx-deployment --cpu-percent=2 --min=2 --max=5
kubectl get hpa
--min = minimum pods always running 14
--max = maximum pods HPA can scale to 14
--cpu-percent = CPU threshold that triggers scaling 15
3. Metric Server
What it is: Collects CPU/memory metrics from all pods and nodes so HPA can act on them. 71617
Why needed: Without it, HPA has no data to determine when to scale. 7
Note: GKE may include it by default; AWS or on-premises requires manual installation. 7
Install command:
kubectl apply -f [https://github.com/.../components.yaml](https://github.com/.../components.yaml)
Important: -f flag means file, NOT force. 181920
Verify installation:
kubectl get apiservices | grep metrics
